const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my gas grill not getting hot?",
    a: "On a propane grill, the most common cause of low heat — a weak flame that won't climb even with every burner on high — is a tripped regulator, also called bypass or the safety trip. The excess-flow safety in the regulator senses a surge (usually from opening the tank valve with the burners on) and throttles gas down to a trickle. The fix is a reset: turn all burners OFF, close the tank valve, disconnect the regulator, open the lid, wait 30 to 60 seconds, reconnect hand-tight, open the tank valve slowly, then light. If it's still weak after a clean reset, the regulator itself has failed and needs replacement. Other causes are clogged burner ports, a nearly empty or very cold tank, or a kinked hose. $89 outdoor diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I reset a gas grill regulator (bypass mode)?",
    a: "Turn all burner knobs OFF. Turn the propane tank valve OFF. Disconnect the regulator coupling from the tank (counter-clockwise). Open the grill lid. Wait about 30 to 60 seconds for the internal safety to release. Reconnect the regulator hand-tight only — no wrench. Slowly open the tank valve a quarter turn, pause a few seconds, then open it fully. Now light the grill normally and bring the burners to high. Some manufacturers add an optional purge step (briefly run the burners on high while disconnected to bleed residual gas); it's optional and sources differ, so the load-bearing sequence is off, off, disconnect, wait, reconnect, slow-open, light. If the flame is still weak after two or three clean resets, replace the regulator."
  },
  {
    q: "My grill only reaches a low temperature even on high — is it the burners?",
    a: "Usually not. When every burner is weak at the same time, the problem is upstream of the burners — almost always the regulator in bypass mode, or a gas-supply issue like a low or cold tank. Burners themselves tend to fail one at a time, not all at once, and that shows up as one weak or uneven zone rather than the whole grill being cool. Reset the regulator first. If only one or two burners are weak, look at clogged burner ports or a spider-web blockage in that burner's venturi tube instead. Our deep dive is on the grill regulator issues page."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I check my grill for a gas leak?",
    a: "Use the soapy-water test — never a flame. Mix roughly one part dish soap to three or four parts water in a spray bottle. With the burners OFF, slowly open the tank valve, then spray the solution on every hose connection and the fittings between the tank valve and the regulator. Watch for bubbles: bubbles mean a leak (bigger bubbles, bigger leak). If you find one, close the valve, tighten the connection, and re-test. Never check for a leak with a match or lighter, and never use a grill you suspect is leaking — shut off the tank, keep away from flames and sparks, and call a professional or your propane supplier."
  },
  {
    q: "What does the $89 outdoor diagnostic cover?",
    a: "The $89 residential outdoor diagnostic gets a technician to your grill to find the real cause of the low heat and give you a written quote, and it's waived when you approve the repair. We check the regulator, tank, and hoses first because that's where most low-flame calls resolve, then the burners, venturi tubes, and orifices. We handle the parts of the job that aren't operator-safe — gas valve, orifice, and manifold work — with EPA 608 Universal certification and a proper leak test. BHGS #A49573."
  }
];

