<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The most common answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Weak flame on every burner? Reset the regulator first.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a propane grill won't get hot — a weak flame that won't climb even with every burner cranked to high — the number-one cause is a tripped regulator, also called bypass mode or the safety trip. The regulator's excess-flow safety senses a gas surge (usually from opening the tank valve while the burners were on) and throttles the gas down to a trickle. It's not a broken grill, and it's an operator-safe reset: turn all burners OFF, close the tank valve, disconnect the regulator, open the lid, wait 30 to 60 seconds, reconnect hand-tight, open the tank valve slowly, then light. That fixes most low-flame grills without a single part.</strong></p>

    <p>If the flame is still weak after a couple of clean resets, the regulator itself is worn out and needs replacing — an inexpensive part. Beyond the regulator, the other real causes of low heat are clogged burner ports, a nearly empty or very cold propane tank, or a kinked hose. This page walks the reset step-by-step, then the rest of the checks, then the gas-safety rules that always come first. For the deeper regulator and natural-gas-conversion detail, see our <a href="/outdoor/grill-repair/regulator-issues/">grill regulator issues</a> page.</p>

    <p><strong>$89 outdoor diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong> BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal #1346255700410. Related symptoms: <a href="/outdoor/grill-repair/temperature-uneven/">uneven temperature</a>, <a href="/outdoor/grill-repair/burner-not-igniting/">burner not igniting</a>. Parent: <a href="/outdoor/grill-repair/">built-in grill repair</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 REGULATOR RESET -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · The regulator reset</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The one-minute fix, step by step.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Turn all burner knobs OFF.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Turn the propane tank valve fully OFF.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Disconnect the regulator</strong> coupling from the tank (counter-clockwise).</li>
      <li><strong>Open the grill lid</strong> — never reset or light with the lid closed.</li>
      <li><strong>Wait about 30 to 60 seconds</strong> for the internal excess-flow valve to release.</li>
      <li><strong>Reconnect the regulator hand-tight</strong> — snug by hand, no wrench, don't over-torque.</li>
      <li><strong>Slowly open the tank valve</strong> — a quarter turn, pause a few seconds, then open it all the way.</li>
      <li><strong>Light the grill</strong> and bring the burners to high. Full, steady flame means the reset worked.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Optional purge step:</strong> some grill makers add a step where you briefly turn the burners to high (with the regulator disconnected) to bleed off residual gas before reconnecting. Sources differ on whether it's needed and on the exact wait time, so treat it as optional — the part that always matters is: burners off, tank off, disconnect, wait, reconnect hand-tight, open the tank slowly, then light.</p>

    <p><strong>Prevention:</strong> always open the tank valve <em>slowly</em> and with the burners <em>off</em>, and turn the burners off before you close the tank — that keeps the pressure balanced and stops the safety from tripping in the first place. If the grill still runs weak after two or three clean resets, the regulator has fatigued and needs replacement.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 OTHER CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · The other causes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">If the reset didn't do it.</h2>

    <ul class="bullet-list">
      <li><strong>Clogged burner ports or a spider-web blockage.</strong> Spiders and insects love to nest in venturi tubes, and grease clogs the burner ports — both starve the flame. You can brush the ports clear and clean the venturi tubes with a venturi brush or pipe cleaner per your grill's manual. (Operator.)</li>
      <li><strong>Low or nearly empty tank.</strong> A tank running low delivers weak pressure. Check the level and swap or refill it. (Operator.)</li>
      <li><strong>Very cold tank.</strong> In cold weather propane vaporizes slowly and the regulator can frost over, cutting output. Keep the cylinder upright, shaded, and not overfilled. (Operator.)</li>
      <li><strong>Kinked, cracked, or aged hose.</strong> Rubber hoses harden and microcrack over the years. Inspect the full length and the fittings; replace a damaged hose assembly. (Operator inspect / replace.)</li>
      <li><strong>Corroded or worn burner tubes.</strong> Burners eventually rust through and won't carry a full flame; the OEM burner part is replaceable, but the gas connections behind it are technician territory.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="tech-note"><strong>Technician only — never DIY:</strong> the gas control <strong>valve internals</strong>, the burner <strong>orifices</strong> (never drill or re-size an orifice yourself), and any fixed <strong>gas-line or manifold</strong> work. A wrong orifice or a nicked gas line risks a leak, carbon monoxide, or fire. A propane-to-natural-gas conversion also belongs to a licensed pro with the OEM kit and a pressure check — not a DIY job. Our techs carry EPA 608 Universal certification for that side of the work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 GAS SAFETY -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · Gas safety first</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The leak test — and the rules that don't bend.</h2>

    <p>Any time you disconnect and reconnect gas, check for leaks with the <strong>soapy-water test — never a flame.</strong> Mix roughly one part dish soap to three or four parts water in a spray bottle. With the burners <strong>off</strong>, slowly open the tank valve and spray the solution on every hose connection and the fittings between the tank valve and the regulator. Bubbles forming anywhere mean a leak — bigger bubbles, bigger leak. Close the valve, tighten the connection, and re-test.</p>

    <p class="callout callout-safety"><strong>Never do these:</strong> never check for a leak with a match, lighter, or any flame. Never keep using a grill you suspect is leaking — shut off the tank valve if it's safe to reach, stay away from flames, sparks, and electrical switches, move clear, and call a professional or your propane supplier. If bubbles keep appearing after you tighten a fitting, stop and get it serviced.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Reset didn't hold, or it's past the operator line.</h2>

    <p>If the regulator reset doesn't hold, the burners are corroded, or the trouble is in the gas valve, an orifice, or the manifold, that's our call. Our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair check the regulator, tank, and hoses first — because that's where most low-flame calls resolve — then the burners and venturi tubes, and we verify manifold pressure and leak-test every fitting on the gas side. We service the pro-tier built-in brands across LA, OC, and Ventura, and we don't sell you burners you don't need.</p>

    <p>More grill help: the deep <a href="/outdoor/grill-repair/regulator-issues/">regulator issues</a> page (bypass, NG regulator drift, LP-to-NG conversion), <a href="/outdoor/grill-repair/temperature-uneven/">uneven temperature</a>, <a href="/outdoor/grill-repair/burner-not-igniting/">burner not igniting</a>, and the <a href="/outdoor/grill-repair/">built-in grill repair</a> hub. <strong>$89 outdoor diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong> <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Gas grill low heat: common questions.</h2>
